sjdevnull@yahoo.com <sjdevnull@yahoo.com> wrote:
>Byron A Jeff wrote:

>> Another useful Elbereth tool is a hard gemstone if you can find one.
>> When you use one to engrave, it'll say "engrave" instead of "write in
>> the dust". Also it's more permanent that dust writing which often get
>> smudged just standing there.

>OTOH, using a gem takes one turn per letter engraved during which time
>you're a sitting duck.  Using your fingers to engrave Elbereth every
>turn (adding to what's there) is usually a lot safer.

The key is not to do it while you're a sitting duck. Engravings with gems
will last longer than writing in the dust.

>If you're next to a tough monster and try the gem thing, it'll get 7
>free turns to attack you.

Like I said do it before. 

Also I'm not a die-hard Elbereth user. Any tough monster in one of my
games would be led to the pet for dispatch.

>Wands of fire and lightning are the best E tools.  Wands of digging and
>athames are next best.

All true. Just adding a hard gem to the list. Remember this is all
very early in the game. So happening across any of these items is
somewhat chancy.

>> I tamed them with food rations and now they are my new pet army. The
>> upshot is that my character is XL9! I have about $10000 to spend and I
>> haven't gotten to the minetown shops yet. I figure I can buy about
>> 5 to 6 points of protection and keep moving until I get more money.
>> The point is that there's no point in staying XL1 if it'll get your
>> character killed.

>This is why the protection racket is such a bad idea--you can easily
>level up and get half the protection at the same time, and you'll get
>the rest pretty quickly.

I'm not so sure. There's a balance between levelling up and monster 
toughness that you have to balance. The extra 9-10 points of AC makes
your character a lot tougher target. So you really do still want to get
the protection AC as quickly as possible without levelling up. On average my characters
are XL2 or so reaching Minetown. Monsters aren't too tough, and the pet
is maxxed out.

>Just playing it straight without any racketeering I'm usually XL 7 or
>so with enough money for half the protection when I get to Minetown,
>and it's not too long before I get the rest.

But I still find trouble in the monsters you come across at XL7. At that
point killer bees, rothes, Mordor and hill orcs are the norm. And they
come in packs. At XL1 or XL2 those monsters don't get generated.

>Whereas if you stay at XL1 it's tough to get enough HP to avoid a
>randomly generated wand of lightning, fire, cold, etc from being an
>instadeath.  And the whole key to surviving the game is to eliminate
>instadeaths mercilessly.

Actually rolling boulder traps are much more likely.

>  Level up fairly early, even if just a few
>times.  (though it _may_ be worth doing the "pray for HP" gambit before
>levelling up).

In fact not levelling up is really hard work. There have been a number
of times I've gained XPs simply by bumping into a goblin, fox, or
gnome zombie. You have to pattern to let your pet lead.

It's just another strategy. As I've pointed out I'm not too hung up on
staying XL1. But until you get the extra AC, the lower your XL, the
lesser level of monster is generated. Frankly a XL9 large cat or dog
truly outmatches any random monster that the RNG will generate with th
PC at XL1 or XL2.

